20The new logs show exactly what is stalling your cluster deployment: the operator is crashing and rebooting, and your Ceph configurations are clashing with a stale or dirty state.Here are the critical errors discovered in the logs and how to fix them:1. The Real Issue: Stale Finalizers and "Modified Object" RacesWhen you see lines like this:Operation cannot be fulfilled on cephfilesystems.ceph.rook.io "dc-lab-ceph-fs": the object has been modified; please apply your changes to the latest version and try againIt means the Rook Operator is trying to update the status of your Ceph resources (kube-rbd block pools and dc-lab-ceph-fs file systems), but another thread or old state hook is mutating them at the exact same time. This triggers a conflict loop, failing reconciliation instantly.2. Orphaned Storage ResourcesThe operator log shows it finding preexisting endpoints:parsing mon endpoints: b=10.233.15.190:6789,a=10.233.52.0:6789Because you have existing finalizers (cephblockpool.ceph.rook.io, cephfilesystem.ceph.rook.io), Kubernetes refuses to let go of old resources, preventing the deployment of clean OSD pods for your virtual disks.Step-by-Step Resolution StrategyYou need to perform a forceful cleanup of the broken custom resources so the operator can start with a fresh slate.Step 1: Force-Delete Block Pools and FilesystemsIf your cluster is stuck in an initialization loop, delete the higher-level storage resources that are triggering the lock:bashkubectl delete cephblockpool kube-rbd -n rook-ceph --timeout=10s
kubectl delete cephfilesystem dc-lab-ceph-fs -n rook-ceph --timeout=10s
kubectl delete cephobjectstore dc-lab-ceph-store -n rook-ceph --timeout=10s
Use code with caution.Step 2: Strip Stuck Finalizers (If Commands Hang)If the commands above freeze, it confirms stuck finalizers. Patch them manually to let Kubernetes delete them:bashkubectl patch cephblockpool kube-rbd -n rook-ceph -p '{"metadata":{"finalizers":null}}' --type=merge
kubectl patch cephfilesystem dc-lab-ceph-fs -n rook-ceph -p '{"metadata":{"finalizers":null}}' --type=merge
kubectl patch cephobjectstore dc-lab-ceph-store -n rook-ceph -p '{"metadata":{"finalizers":null}}' --type=merge
Use code with caution.Step 3: Verify the Mon ConfigurationThe logs mention Monitor a and b. Ensure that these pods (rook-ceph-mon-a, rook-ceph-mon-b) are actually running:bashkubectl get pods -n rook-ceph -l app=rook-ceph-mon
Use code with caution.If these pods are stuck in CrashLoopBackOff or Error, the operator can never contact Ceph to prepare your /dev/vdc or /dev/vdb disks.Step 4: Restart the OperatorOnce the resource locks are broken, force the operator to spin up fresh configuration controllers:bashkubectl rollout restart deployment rook-ceph-operator -n rook-ceph
Use code with caution.After doing this, check if the operator progresses past the parsing mon endpoints stage. If it gets stuck on the monitors again, can you check the status of your rook-ceph-mon pods?
